{ [%csl Gc2][%cal Gc2c4] }
1. c4 { [%cal Gd7d5] } 1... d5 2. cxd5 Qxd5 3. g4 { Bring out the black bishop. If you don't, it leads to a stronger position for black. } 3... Bxg4 4. Qa4 { That's the one. You should always play this. You'll see why as we progress through the lesson. However, just keep in mind that Qa4 gives white maximum mobility. } (4. Qc2 { This isn't necessarily bad, but there's a better move. }) (4. Qb3 { The queen will take, and then white has a terrible position. } { [%cal Gd5b3] }) 4... Bxe2 { Could black have taken a piece with the queen instead? Yes. Qxa2 is a loss for black. So is Qxh1. And so is Qxd2. Are they easy wins for white to find? No, but people rarely play them. Bxe2 is played 85-90% of the time. Still, we will revisit these losing moves for black later in the lesson. } 5. Nxe2 { Taking with the knight is the strongest move. Other moves are playable, but white's position will be weaker. This is where the intro ends. Black can take on a2, d2, and h1. We will look at all of these lines and the playable variations.
NOTE: All of these variations assume black plays 4...Bxe2 (and doesn't play a queen move instead). Variations in strategy arise if black leaves the bishop on the board, but most ideas still apply. } (5. Bxe2 { Playable, but black is going to leave white with an open center and some dangerous bishops. }) (5. Kxe2 { Playable, but black can put white in an uncomfortable spot with a less certain queen race. }) (5. Qxe8 { You leave black the option of taking on f1. Not necessarily a death sentence yet, but why give him the choice? }) *


[Event "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess: 5. Nxe2 Qxa2 (6. Qxe8 Qxa1 variation)"]
