{ This time, black's queen takes on b1, which is by far the least common choice for black. }
6... Qxb1 7. Rxa7 { Mnemonic aid: If the queen tries to "sit down" beside the rook, run away as far as you can! Black has to take with the rook, which he does more than half the time. If he doesn't, just capture the queen on b2 or c1 and the win will be obvious. } (7. Qxf7 { This is fine to play. In many of these variations, f7 is our "safe space"--when in doubt, Qxf7 is generally safe. However, with this specific variation, there is an easier win that doesn't require an extended queen race. }) 7... Rxa7 8. Qxf7 { That's right, move to your safe space on f7. } (8. Qxf8 { No, you need to keep that bishop on the board }) (8. Qxe7 { This still maintains white's winning position, but it's a more complicated line than the correct move. }) 8... Qxc1 9. Qxg7 { Black can't take with the bishop, obviously. He also can't take on b2 because then Qxg8, bringing out the rook. All he can do it continue the queen race with Qxe1. } 9... Qxe1 10. Qxh7 { Taking on h7 forces the black queen to take on f2. Otherwise, you recapture the queen and that rook comes out. Qxh7 is the best move because it reduces black's options to a single move. } (10. Qxh8 { This works, but a different move leads to an easier finish for white. }) 10... Qxf2 11. Qxh8 { Yes--you want to continue the queen race at this point. If you keep your queen alive, black can't do anything. } (11. Qxe7 { This also works, but it leads to a more complicated line in my opinion. }) 11... Qxh2 { It doesn't matter what black does. Qxh2, Qxf1, and Qxe2 are all options, but white will make the same next move in all cases. } 12. Qxg8 Qxe2 { Black is not going to take the rook on h1 here, because then it's a straight run through all the rest of white's pieces. Taking on e2 appears to give him options (but it doesn't). } 13. Qxf8 Qxd2 { What if black would have taken the bishop instead? Well, play it out--still a quick loss. } 14. Qxb8 Qxb2 15. Qxb7 *


