[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "5. Nxe2 Qxa2 (6. Qxe8 Qxb2 variation)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/sGLcFfkx"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn2Qbnr/ppp1pppp/8/8/8/8/qP1PNP1P/RNB1KB1R b - - 0 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.21"]
[UTCTime "04:35:30"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

6... Qxb2 { This is the most commonly played 6th move for black. It is also slightly more difficult to play for white than the other lines. Most often, white plays Rxa7, but I think Qxf7 is a little easier to play for white. } 7. Qxf7 { If black plays Qxb1, then white has an easy win--just remember: if the black queen tries to sit beside your rook, run away (Rxa7)! Black's queen will be stuck with one option, Qxc1, which allows white to capture the queen and put an end to things quickly. If black doesn't play Qxb1? Well, it gets a little tricky. We will examine black's most common and strongest move first. } 7... Qxd2 { This is black's most common move. We aren't going to play the most efficient line, which would be white wins in 10 because it's a complicated queen race. You could memorize it, but instead, take black's queen and you have a much simpler win in 12! Which piece will you use? } 8. Bxd2 { Right! There aren't any great moves for black here. Most people play a5, which is an easy win for white. Watch out for the tricky a6! This one is easy enough that I'll make it into a quiz in the next chapter. } (8. Qxg7 { Right! What if black takes with the bishop? You take the queen and black will have to take the rook in the corner--easy win after that. Black HAS to keep the queen race going with Qxe1. } 8... Qxe1 { At this point, you're looking to extend the queen race--just move where black can't take your queen. } 9. Qxh8 { No matter what black does, you're going to avoid letting him take your queen. Things are...less straightforward if black decides to take the knight. You'll have to think hard. } (9. Qxf8 { This is wrong because white doesn't have a move to keep the queen alive after this. No matter where white moves the queen, black can capture it. This is actually a loss for white. Where can you move that will give you more options? }) (9. Qxg8 { Did you forget about your rook on a1? You can't let black capture that queen at the moment, because then it's a race of rook against queen, and a loss for white. }) 9... Qxf2 10. Qxg8) (8. Rxa7 { This works, but it gives white a more complicated path to victory. Good instinct--we will play this move eventually. }) (8. Qxf8 { Again, this works according to Stockfish, but it is super complicated. and one wrong move puts white in a losing position. }) 8... a6 *


