[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "5. Nxe2 Qxd2 (6. Qxe8 Qxc1 variation)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/RoXcm5K0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn2Qbnr/ppp1pppp/8/8/8/8/PP1qNP1P/RNB1KB1R b - - 0 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.25"]
[UTCTime "22:09:57"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

6... Qxc1 { This is the second option for black from left to right. Instead of playing Qxb2, this time black plays Qxc1. This is actually black's best move, mate in 24 for white. The cool thing about this one is that white gets to end the queen race immediately. } { [%cal Ge2c1] } 7. Nxc1 { The most common move for black here is b5, but we know that's not right. Check the next chapter for a quiz where black plays b5 and see if you can solve it. Keep in mind: black almost never plays the ideal line. This chapter covers the most difficult way for white to win--it'll typically be a lot easier. } { [%csl Gb7] } 7... Nd7 { Nd7 is black's strongest move here. Tempting to sack the queen right away so you can get rid of that bishop...but not so fast. If you can remember white's next move, you got this every time! } 8. Qxf7 { Correct! Black is prevented from playing b5 and bringing out the bishop because if white plays Qxg7, then black's dark square bishop is coming out to gobble some pieces for an easy white win. } 8... g6 { From here on, it's basically an engine proof. I would never be able to remember this line, but like I said earlier, it's unlikely you'll find yourself in this situation. Focus on the easier wins, because they occur with much greater frequency. } 9. Qxe7 Nxe7 10. Bd3 Rg8 11. Bxg6 hxg6 12. f4 c5 13. Kf2 Nc8 14. Ke3 Ne5 15. fxe5 c4 16. a3 Bxa3 17. Rxa3 Nd6 18. Rxa7 Rxa7 19. exd6 Ra2 20. Nxa2 c3 21. bxc3 Rb8 22. Nb4 Rg8 23. Kf4 g5 24. Kxg5 Rxg5 25. Nd5 Rxd5 26. h3 Rxd6 27. Nd2 Rxd2 28. Rh2 Rxh2 29. h4 Rxh4 30. c4 Rxc4# *


