[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "5. Nxe2 Qxh1 (8...Nxe7 variation)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/kfaTsegA"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn2kbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3q4/Q7/8/PP1PNP1P/RNB1KB1R b - - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.24"]
[UTCTime "00:39:12"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

5... Qxh1 { So...this is a win in 38 for white. That's not great news for learning this line. :) But don't worry, it won't be as bad as you think. Black's chances become very limited very quickly. We start normally, with Qxe8. } { [%cal Ga4e8] } 6. Qxe8 Qxf1 { Whether the black queen takes on f1 or h2, white will play the same move: go to your safe space. } 7. Qxf7 Qxe1 8. Qxe7 { Right! This is a tough one to find, but now white almost certainly has a quick win locked up. The ONE move black can play that leads to mate in 27 (instead of 10) is the extremely rare Nxe7, capturing white's queen. But almost no one ever does that because it looks like suicide. Provided black takes with the queen instead, the win is actually pretty easy for white. We will look at that scenario in the next chapter. } 8... Nxe7 9. d4 { Did you find that one? Not easy! } 9... Qxc1 10. Nxc1 c5 11. dxc5 b6 12. cxb6 axb6 13. b4 { Getting that pawn out in front of the bishop will payoff big time later on in the game. } 13... Rxa2 14. Nxa2 { At this point, it's really tough to know what to do. Black's best move is b5. However, he could play a lot of different things here that lead to a difficult endgame. It's a win in 20, but it won't be easy. } 14... b5 15. Nd2 Nd7 16. Nc1 Nb6 17. Rb1 Nc4 18. Nxc4 bxc4 19. Ra1 { b2 also works. Like I said, this is a difficult line. Remember, no one ever plays it. Look at the barely covered bishop on f8 that's pointed at white's unprotected pawn. Exploit it! } 19... h5 20. Ra2 g5 21. f4 gxf4 22. Rd2 { Now you got it. } 22... Nd5 23. Rxd5 Bxb4 24. Rxh5 Rxh5 25. h3 Rxh3 26. Nd3 Rxd3# *


[Event "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess: 5. Nxe2 Qxh1 (8...Qxe2 variation)"]
[Date "2025.11.24"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "5. Nxe2 Qxh1 (8...Qxe2 variation)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/beQ9ACvX"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn3bnr/ppp1Q1pp/8/8/8/8/PP1PNP1P/RNB1q3 b - - 0 8"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.24"]
[UTCTime "21:50:08"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Just like in the previous chapter, white plays Qxe7. Assuming black doesn't play Nxe7, he's going to take one of white's pieces with the queen. In ALL cases, white plays Qxg7. The idea is to open up his rook, which is pointed at the unprotected h pawn. Let's see what happens if black plays Qxe2. }
8... Qxe2 9. Qxg7 Qxf2 10. Qxh7 { Now you see it? Look at that wide open bishop on f8! That's gonna be a problem for black. What if black takes h2? Take back with the queen and bring down the rook. What if black takes on d2? Take back with the knight and bring down the rook. } 10... Qxd2 11. Nxd2 Rxh7 12. Ne4 Rxh2 13. Nf2 Rxf2 14. Bd2 Rxd2 15. a4 Rxb2 16. Ra2 Rxa2 17. a5 Rxa5# *


[Event "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess: 5. Nxe2 Qxh1 (8...Qxc1 variation)"]
[Date "2025.11.24"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "5. Nxe2 Qxh1 (8...Qxc1 variation)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/UwsKzEZG"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn3bnr/ppp1Q1pp/8/8/8/8/PP1PNP1P/RNB1q3 b - - 0 8"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.24"]
[UTCTime "22:15:11"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ The only difference from the previous chapter is that here black plays Qxc1 instead of Qxe2. If black plays either of the other two possible moves (Qxd2 or Qxf2), it's the same as the previous chapter. White will play Qxg7, leaving the bishop wide open and preparing to release black's h-rook. }
8... Qxc1 9. Qxg7 { Now you can see the difference that Qxc1 made: black has more possibilities for continuing the queen race than he had with the other moves. Don't worry, he won't get away with it! Let's play with black continuing the queen race as long as possible. } 9... Qxb2 10. Qxh7 { That's right--open up that rook! Wait...what if black plays Qxb1 or Qxa2, opening white's rook? Just take the queen. Black will lose the rook vs. rook race every time. } 10... Qxa1 11. Qxc7 Qxa2 { This is a crucial point because black can take the knight on b1 and there's nothing left in its path after that. Either of white's moves works, but I think Qxb8 is easier to calculate. } 12. Qxb8 Qxd2 13. Nxd2 Rxh2 14. Qxa8 Rxf2 15. Qxf8 { What if black takes the white queen? Doesn't matter at this point. } 15... Rxe2 16. Qxg8 Rxd2 17. Qd8 Rxd8# *


