[Event "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess: Black Deviation #1 4. Qa4 Qxh1 5. Qxa7 Bxe2"]
[Date "2025.11.22"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "Black Deviation #1 4. Qa4 Qxh1 5. Qxa7 Bxe2"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/laEHOo1P"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn2kbnr/ppp1pppp/8/8/Q5b1/8/PP1PPP1P/RNB1KBNq w - - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.22"]
[UTCTime "15:10:08"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Okay, big difference here that deviates from the standard opening line we've played so far. Black has not played Bxe2, but has instead chosen to take h1 with the queen. Black won't do that with his other queen options (Qxa2 & Qxd2) because they lead to quick losses. Playing 4...Qxh1 happens in a paltry 3% of games. Remember, in 86% of games, black is going to take with the bishop before committing to a direction for his queen. We will look at all of these possibilities, though, in the interest of being complete. }
5. Qxa7 { It's a win in 14. The most popular next move is for black to finally take with the bishop. In which case, white should play Bxe2! That veers away from our standard opening where we'd normally take with the knight. Just remember: if the black bishop is still in the middle of the board, you're in a "special situation." Thing'll be a bit different. } { [%cal Gg4e2] } 5... Bxe2 { Taking with the bishop is the most commonly played move, so we'll look at that first. Black's other two options, Qxh2 and Qxg1, are covered in Chapter 14 (just after the next quiz). } 6. Bxe2 { You take with the bishop--this ONLY applies in games where black plays 5...Qxh1 (normally you take with the knight). It is very highly probable black will play Qxh2 or Qxg1 here. If black unexpectedly decides to play Rxa7, it's a whole different line for white, which really complicates everything (see next chapter). Let's examine the probable next move. } 6... Qxg1 7. Qxb7 { From here on out, you're just playing keep away. Capture black's queen if he plays Qxf2, then you have black rook vs. white queen in a race. } { [%cal Gb7c7] } 7... Qxe1 (7... Qxh2 8. Qxc7) 8. Qxc7 { Look at black's 4 possible moves. For 3 of them, white can't immediately recapture (disregarding white capturing with 9. Qxc1, which would clearly be disadvantageous). Only Qxd2 allows white to immediately capture black's queen and get in a race with the rook. If black gives you that opportunity, take it. Otherwise, keep playing keep away. } 8... Qxc1 { It is safe to move into a spot where the white queen can be captured because if that happens, white cannot immediately capture black's queen and it is forced to move again while the black rook remains open. } { [%cal Gc7b8] } 9. Qxb8 { If black lets you take the queen on the next move, take it. You can calculate the win easily after that. Let's turn it into a quiz (see next chapter). For now, let's deal with black continuing the queen race with Qxb2. What should you do? Keep away! } 9... Qxb2 10. Qxa8 Qxd2 11. Qxe8 { That's right--play keep away until the end. White will win this! } 11... Qxe2 12. Qxf8 { Qxf7 works too. } (12. Qxe7 Nxe7) 12... Qxf2 13. Qxg7 { There are other ways to win, even if white gives up the queen. But keep it simple--just play keep away and you'll win. No need to look for alternatives. } (13. Qxg8 Qxh2 14. Qxg7 Qxa2 15. Qxf7 Qxa1 16. Qxe7 Qxb1 17. Qxh7 Qxh7#) 13... Qxh2 14. Qxh8 Qxa2 15. Qxh7 Qxb1 16. Qxf7 Qxa1 17. Qxe7 Nxe7# *


