[UTCTime "02:39:00"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ This time, we'll look at what happens if white plays Qxa7 and black answers with Qxg1 (or Qxh2, which will turn out the same from whit's perspective). Black's idea is to extend the queen race--basically, he's trying to make things complicated for white until he can find an opportunity. } { [%cal Gh1g1,Ga4a7] }
5. Qxa7 Qxg1 *


[Event "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess: Black deviation #3 5. Ne2 Qxh1 (6. Qxa7 Rxa7 variation)"]
[Date "2025.11.23"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "Black deviation #3 5. Ne2 Qxh1 (6. Qxa7 Rxa7 variation)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/RBiwVJ1a"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn2kbnr/Qpp1pppp/8/8/8/8/PP1PBP1P/RNB1K1Nq b - - 0 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.23"]
[UTCTime "22:45:06"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Black playing Rxa7 is quite uncommon, but it introduces a line that prevents us from playing our "keep away" strategy. It's actually pretty difficult to remember because white has to make a counterintuitive move immediately after its queen is captured. However, what you can remember is that you are essentially doing cleanup. Black has lost, and you can lead his loose queen and rook through all white's pieces if you're careful. } { [%cal Ga8a7] }
6... Rxa7 7. b4 { That's it! The counterintuitive move is to play b4 here. } 7... Qxg1 8. f4 { Yes. This is to avoid having to capture the queen on f2 with the king--you're just keeping that queen gobbling up pieces. } 8... Qxe1 9. Ba3 { Great! If black takes the knight, take the queen with white's rook. If black takes the bishop with his rook, take back with the knight and then the black queen can't take anything that results in its immediate capture. And cleanup is pretty easy after this. Easy enough to...make into a quiz! See next chapter. } 9... Qxe2 *


