[Event "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess: When black blunders early: Part 1 (4...Qxa2)"]
[Date "2025.11.21"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Antichess"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Let's Learn the Wizard Opening - antichess"]
[ChapterName "When black blunders early: Part 1 (4...Qxa2)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/f3NuTHGo/wxlNyMjY"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Dy1ar"]
[FEN "rn2kbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3q4/Q5b1/8/PP1PPP1P/RNB1KBNR b - - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.11.21"]
[UTCTime "22:02:59"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Here, we return to what happens if black veers away from the standard opening and fails to play Bxe2. Instead, black will play a queen move: Qxa2, Qxd2, or Qxh1. Let's move left to right and begin with Qxa2 }
4... Qxa2 5. Qxa2 { That's the one! Rxa2 also works, but (to me anyway) the picture of what to do next is clearer when you play Qxa2. Now black only has one choice. } (5. Qxe8 { Nope, this blows the win for white. Aaaaaand it's the most commonly played move at 66%! }) 5... Bxe2 6. Qxa7 { Now, what happens if black plays Rxa7? White takes the bishop on e2 (doesn't even matter which piece white uses!) and black suddenly has a loose rook pointed at the back row. Not good for black. Black is going to want to play Bxf8 and hope to get lucky. } 6... Bxf1 7. Qxb7 { Great! That bishop on f1 isn't going anywhere, so black now has to bring the rook down. } 7... Rxa1 8. Qxb8 { Why not take the bishop to simplify things? You could do that, but I think it leads to a slightly more difficult cleanup. This will already be difficult enough. In this position, white can use the strategy of "just don't move into a place where black can take the queen--EVER" and it works. If you take the bishop there will be some exchange variations you need to think about. } 8... Rxb1 9. Qxe8 Rxb2 { If black moves along the back rank and takes c1 instead, things are over quickly. The next chapter is a quiz where you can find that easy win. It's harder to play when black takes on b2, but just follow the advice: don't let him take your queen! } 10. Qxf8 { Qxf7 works too. } (10. Qxf7 { This works, but we're playing Qxf8 here }) 10... Rxd2 { No choices for black. } 11. Qxf7 Rxf2 { No choices for black. } 12. Qxg7 Rxh2 { No choices for black. } 13. Qxh8 Rxh1 { No choices for black. } 14. Kxf1 { Great! That's the one little tricky part. Remember, up until now, black has had no choice but to move in a straight line through your pieces. All you had to do was keep away. Now there are several ways to win. You can count it out. } 14... Rxg1 15. Qxh7 Rxf1 16. Qxg8 Rxc1 17. Qg1 Rxg1# *


